{"name":"Concept Beige","hex":"#d5bda3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"concept","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An abstract and general idea; an abstraction.","Understanding retained in the mind, from experience, reasoning and imagination; a generalization (generic, basic form), or abstraction (mental impression), of a particular set of instances or occurrences (specific, though different, recorded manifestations of the concept)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To conceive; to dream up."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/concept"},{"term":"beige","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour, variously defined from a pale brown, to a yellow greyish off-white.","Debeige; a kind of woollen or mixed dress goods."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a slightly yellowish gray colour, as that of unbleached wool.","Comfortably dull and unadventurous, in a way that suggests middle-class suburbia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beige"}],"entries":[{"title":"Concept","blocks":[{"text":"A concept is a fundamental unit of cognition that classifies entities and encodes shared features. Concepts make it possible to form and combine ideas, draw inferences, and refer to external objects. They act as the meanings of words and play a central role in many cognitive processes, including perception, memory, and reasoning. Researchers distinguish different types of concepts based on their internal structure, mode of acquisition, and domain. These include simple and complex concepts, learned and innate concepts, concrete and abstract concepts, and natural and logical concepts."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concept"},{"title":"Beige","blocks":[{"text":"Beige is variously described as a pale sandy fawn color, a grayish tan, a light-grayish yellowish brown, or a pale to grayish yellow. It takes its name from French, where the word originally meant natural wool that has been neither bleached nor dyed, hence also the color of natural wool."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Saint_Anthony_church_in_Chamboret_07.jpg/330px-Saint_Anthony_church_in_Chamboret_07.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beige"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/CHEZY_QUEVANNE_Portrait_of_a_mother_and_her_son%2C_1805.jpg/500px-CHEZY_QUEVANNE_Portrait_of_a_mother_and_her_son%2C_1805.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/CHEZY_QUEVANNE_Portrait_of_a_mother_and_her_son%2C_1805.jpg/1280px-CHEZY_QUEVANNE_Portrait_of_a_mother_and_her_son%2C_1805.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/CHEZY_QUEVANNE_Portrait_of_a_mother_and_her_son%2C_1805.jpg/500px-CHEZY_QUEVANNE_Portrait_of_a_mother_and_her_son%2C_1805.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACHEZY_QUEVANNE_Portrait_of_a_mother_and_her_son%2C_1805.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Ludwig_Wittgenstein.jpg/500px-Ludwig_Wittgenstein.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Ludwig_Wittgenstein.jpg/1280px-Ludwig_Wittgenstein.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Ludwig_Wittgenstein.jpg/500px-Ludwig_Wittgenstein.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ludwig Wittgenstein challenged the classical theory, arguing that concepts are usually based on family resemblance without precise underlying definitions.[35]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALudwig_Wittgenstein.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Jerry_Fodor_in_2007.jpg/500px-Jerry_Fodor_in_2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Jerry_Fodor_in_2007.jpg/500px-Jerry_Fodor_in_2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Jerry Fodor proposed conceptual atomism, arguing that lexical concepts are simple units without internal structure.[49]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJerry_Fodor_in_2007.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/John_Locke_%28cropped%3B_square%29.jpg/500px-John_Locke_%28cropped%3B_square%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/John_Locke_%28cropped%3B_square%29.jpg/500px-John_Locke_%28cropped%3B_square%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"John Locke held that the infant mind is a blank slate that depends on sensory experience to acquire concepts.[58]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohn_Locke_(cropped%3B_square).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Eleanor_Rosch_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Eleanor_Rosch_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Eleanor_Rosch_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Eleanor_Rosch_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Eleanor_Rosch_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Eleanor_Rosch_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Eleanor Rosch suggested prototype theory as an alternative to the classical theory.[79]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEleanor_Rosch_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/Jean_Piaget_in_Ann_Arbor_%28cropped%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/Jean_Piaget_in_Ann_Arbor_%28cropped%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Jean Piaget analyzed how children acquire concepts in a series of stages moving from concrete to abstract reasoning.[102]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJean_Piaget_in_Ann_Arbor_(cropped).png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/2/2f/Rudolf_Carnap_1930_cropped.jpeg/500px-Rudolf_Carnap_1930_cropped.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/2/2f/Rudolf_Carnap_1930_cropped.jpeg/1280px-Rudolf_Carnap_1930_cropped.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/2/2f/Rudolf_Carnap_1930_cropped.jpeg/500px-Rudolf_Carnap_1930_cropped.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rudolf Carnap proposed conceptual engineering as a philosophical method to create precise concepts.[115]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARudolf_Carnap_1930_cropped.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/1206_FMRI.jpg/330px-1206_FMRI.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/1206_FMRI.jpg/330px-1206_FMRI.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"fMRI is a neuroimaging technique that can measure regional brain activity associated with conceptual processes.[130]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1206_FMRI.jpg"}],"sounds":{"conc